-- sprint unmask-arg-tag-dispatch S-5 — enumeration cell "an unsigned top-level HOF"
-- (`apply2 g x = g x`), and the POSITIVE CONTROL for the primitive-head residual
-- S-4's report (Note 3) measured: routing a DEFINING `impl Speak Int` through a
-- top-level unsigned HOF instead of a generalized LOCAL dispatches correctly on every
-- engine, exit 0. This is the sibling that proves the emitter is NOT uniformly blind to
-- primitive receivers — the gap the neighbouring `main.mdk`/`localdefine`-shaped
-- residuals hit (see S-5's report Notes) is specific to the arg-tag chain the LOCAL
-- route emits, because an unsigned top-level function's parameter dispatches through
-- the DICT channel (ordinary dict-passing polymorphism), never through arg-tag
-- discrimination at all — so `Int` owning no cell tag never becomes a problem here.
--
-- Ground truth, derived from the source, NOT captured: `impl Speak Int` DEFINES
-- `speak _ = "int"` (overrides the "woof" default); `impl Speak Cat` defines "meow".
-- `apply2 speak 5` forwards `speak` (as a dict-passed value) applied to `5 : Int`,
-- giving "int"; `apply2 speak Cat` gives "meow". Printed in source-call order:
-- int|meow.
--
-- Measured: check/run/build+binary all agree at `int|meow` — clean accept.
--
-- Verdict: ACCEPT.
interface Speak a where
  speak : a -> String
  speak _ = "woof"

data Cat = Cat

impl Speak Int where
  speak _ = "int"

impl Speak Cat where
  speak _ = "meow"

apply2 g x = g x

main = println (apply2 speak 5 ++ "|" ++ apply2 speak Cat)
